WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.045 --> 00:00:01.680
THE INFLUENCE OF SEQUOIA:
Do you know what the similarity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:08.700
between Apple, Google, Nvidia, Cisco, and Oracle&nbsp;
is? Well yes, they’re all multi hundred billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:14.040
dollar Fortune 100 companies, but they&nbsp;
were also all funded by Sequoia Capital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:21.240
Sequoia has also funded Dashlane, Doordash,&nbsp;
Github, Instagram, PayPal, Reddit, SpaceX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.400
Square, WhatsApp, Yahoo, YouTube, Zoom&nbsp;
and all of these other companies as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:34.560
In fact, the combined market cap of all Sequoia&nbsp;
Capital companies is a whopping $3.3 trillion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:41.460
that was as of July of 2019. Since then Apple&nbsp;
alone has reached a market cap of $3 trillion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:47.520
so it’s safe to say that Sequoia's aggregated&nbsp;
market cap is well over $5 trillion today even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.520
with the stock market crash. And all of those are&nbsp;
just a compilation of Sequoia’s known investments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.640
Sequoia has dozens of undisclosed investments&nbsp;
that will likely stay undisclosed indefinitely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:07.620
Considering this, many would argue that Sequoia is&nbsp;
by far the most successful VC firm of all time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:14.220
that’s not a very controversial statement, but&nbsp;
it’s not all sunshines and rainbows. While it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.680
cool to say that you’ve been involved in all of&nbsp;
these companies, the reality is that to get here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:28.560
there’s way more failures. In fact, 75% of all&nbsp;
VC backed startups fail, so for every Apple they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.740
score, they have to deal with 3 wanna be Apple’s&nbsp;
who end up consuming tens of millions each. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.140
that’s not even the worst part. The worst part&nbsp;
is that when you’re making so many investments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.640
it’s only a matter of time until you&nbsp;
invest is someone who’s truly evil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.880
Their most recent slip up was none other than&nbsp;
FTX in which they had a total of $150 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.180
Some of their other controversial and borderline&nbsp;
fraudulent investments include BharatPe, Trell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:04.260
and Zillingo. But, despite the roaring highs&nbsp;
and humiliating lows, it seems like Sequoia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:11.340
has cracked the code. They’ve been operating for&nbsp;
over 50 years, they’ve invested in 1800 companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.820
and they’ve essentially funded&nbsp;
the entirety of silicon valley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.760
So, here’s the story of Sequoia Capital:&nbsp;
the architects of Silicon Valley.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.700
DON VALENTINE:&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:34.800
Taking a look back, the story of Sequoia Capital&nbsp;
traces its roots back to a stern man named Don&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:41.880
Valentine. Don was born on June 26, 1932 in&nbsp;
Manhattan New York during the worst of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:47.520
Great Depression. Don didn’t have a very strong&nbsp;
background either. His father was a regular old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.680
milkman and his mother was a homemaker, so&nbsp;
grit and hardship was something that he got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:58.860
used to very early on. He woud end up attended&nbsp;
Fordham University where he majored in chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:04.560
before moving to Southern California in the&nbsp;
1950s. This was during the height of the Cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.060
War so the defense industry was popping off.&nbsp;
So naturally, Don would get a job as a sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:16.020
engineer at Raytheon, but he didn’t really want&nbsp;
to be here. You see, Don lived by the ideology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:22.560
that the past is wrong. If you want to make truly&nbsp;
great investments, you have to do something that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:29.100
not part of the past, to do something entirely&nbsp;
different. From Don’s perspective, the something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:34.620
that was completely different was obvious, it&nbsp;
was silicon wafers. He didn’t really understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.480
the technical side of these gadgets, but he&nbsp;
knew he wanted to be a part of this industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:45.840
So, he joined Fairchild Semiconductor as one of&nbsp;
their first salesmen and ended up becoming a star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:53.280
He basically built out the entire sales department&nbsp;
over the next 7 years, and he did such a great job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.900
that he would be offered a executive role at&nbsp;
one of Fairchild’s spinoff companies: National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:03.840
Semiconductor. He would happily take the job, but&nbsp;
it wasn’t long until he started having arguments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.420
with his superiors. You see, Don felt that it&nbsp;
was stupid that they were selling boatloads of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.240
wafers to certain companies but not investing in&nbsp;
said companies. He tried to convince leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:22.140
to try their hand at investing, but the answer was&nbsp;
always no. At first Don accepted their reluctance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:28.140
but the more time that passed, the more he felt&nbsp;
that he was missing out. Don started feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:33.840
that the next something was no longer silicon&nbsp;
wafers, but the companies that were built on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:39.660
these wafers. So, Don began investing his own&nbsp;
money into up and coming tech companies which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:45.120
caught the attention of Capital Group. Capital&nbsp;
wasn’t familiar with the tech scene or startups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.400
they were primarily a mutual fund company.&nbsp;
But, while they didn’t have expertise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:57.600
what they did have was money and they wanted&nbsp;
Don to invest this money. With that Don would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.480
quit his job and create Sequoia Capital in&nbsp;
1972 using $3 million worth of funding mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:10.080
from Capital. The term Sequoia alludes to a local&nbsp;
tree that’s known to live thousands of years. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:17.400
he chose quite an ambitious name but Don had&nbsp;
to now walk the walk. Fortunately for Don, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:24.480
wasn’t too hard as his first investment itself&nbsp;
would be $600,000 in Atari. Atari would go on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:31.800
be sold for $28 million to Warner just a few years&nbsp;
later, so it was no doubt a great investment, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:37.620
there was something that made the Atari investment&nbsp;
magnitudes better. If you know your Apple history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:44.580
you probably know that Steve Jobs used to work at&nbsp;
Atari. I think you can guess what happened next.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.000
HITTING THE JACKPOT:&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:58.440
Don wasn’t the first person that Steve approached.&nbsp;
The first person Steve approached was actually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:04.800
founder of Atari: Nolan Bushnell. He offered&nbsp;
Nolan 33% of the company for 50 grand. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.440
Nolan was tempted, he ended up declining and&nbsp;
suggested that Steve talk to Don. Don was sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:16.620
almost immediately. He says that he’s only met two&nbsp;
visionaries throughout his entire life. The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:22.920
one was Robert Noyce, a cofounder of Fairchild&nbsp;
and later Intel. The second was Steve Jobs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.300
But, by the time Steve reached out to Don, they&nbsp;
already had operations underway and Steve had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:41.580
wisened up regarding how much Apple was really&nbsp;
worth. So, Don had to invest a much more hefty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:48.660
$150,000 into Apple. It’s not clear how big of a&nbsp;
stake this gave him, but that didn’t really matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:55.140
as he would end up selling just 2 years later for&nbsp;
$6 million. That’s obviously a phenomenal return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:04.020
but Don didn’t exactly want to sell. The problem&nbsp;
was that Don’s investors needed their money back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:10.740
for tax purposes so Don was forced to sell. This&nbsp;
was quite a shame as Don literally had the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:17.100
world’s largest company in his hand, but this&nbsp;
taught Don a valuable lesson. Never accept money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:23.280
from non tax-exempt institutions. Don wasn’t too&nbsp;
disappointed though. He felt that the computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:29.520
revolution was just getting started and that there&nbsp;
was plenty of opportunity left. With that, Don&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.400
would turn around and invest in Electronic Arts in&nbsp;
1982. He would follow this up with an investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:43.080
into Oracle in 1983. These were obviously great&nbsp;
investments that performed exceptionally well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:49.500
but Don couldn’t help but feel that he just didn’t&nbsp;
have enough skin in the game. Sure, he was pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.940
good an investing a few hundred thousand dollars&nbsp;
and making a few million or maybe even tens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:59.880
millions. But, there was no way that Sequoia would&nbsp;
reach the hundreds of millions or billions if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:04.320
they stuck to this strategy. So, Don started&nbsp;
looking for companies in which he could gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:10.980
a much larger stake. This took quite some time,&nbsp;
but he would eventually find his target: Cisco.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:17.340
In the late 1980s, Cisco was in dire need of&nbsp;
money. They had already asked Sequoia for money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:24.480
but less than a year later, they needed even more&nbsp;
money. Cisco was desperate and lets just say Don&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:31.680
would seize this opportunity. He offered them $2.5&nbsp;
million for 32% of the company. Looking back, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.980
was definitely a predatory offer but Cisco had no&nbsp;
choice but to accept if they wanted to survive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:44.040
This actually worked out super well for Cisco&nbsp;
as Don would bring his expertise in management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:49.740
and sales to Cisco which allowed them to&nbsp;
become the world’s largest company by 2000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:55.320
As for the founders, it didn’t work out so&nbsp;
great for them but that’s a wholenother story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:05.580
At the peak, Cisco was worth $569 billion so&nbsp;
let’s just say, Sequoia made a crap ton of money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:13.440
Sequoia was now the world’s most well known and&nbsp;
most respected VC firm, but they were just getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:19.080
started as they would turn around and invest all&nbsp;
of their profits from Cisco into dotcom favorites.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:25.620
BECOMING THE GOAT:&nbsp;

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:31.380
Some of the last investments that Don personally&nbsp;
led at Sequoia was Nvidia in 1993 and Yahoo in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:38.820
1995 but this was for the best. You see, Don&nbsp;
was extremely self aware and he acknowledged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:44.160
that modern companies were way beyond his&nbsp;
expertise. He was able to somewhat keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:49.500
up as long as companies were actually creating&nbsp;
physical products like Apple, Cisco, and Nvidia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.540
He could help with manufacturing,&nbsp;
suppliers, scaling, and logistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.540 --> 00:10:01.260
But, more and more companies were starting to have&nbsp;
no physical product whatsoever, and this was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:07.560
clear sign to Don that it was time for him to step&nbsp;
down. In 1996, Don would hand over Sequoia to two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:13.620
of his most trusted executives: Doug Leone and&nbsp;
Michael Moritz. These two were nothing less that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:19.560
absolute professionals. They would not only run&nbsp;
Sequoia well but they would run it exactly how Don&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:25.380
had envisioned. For example, in the mid 1990s, the&nbsp;
duo started feeling that much of the opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:31.680
was overseas. At first, they considered just doing&nbsp;
it themselves, but then they thought about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:38.100
Don would approach the situation. If it wasn’t his&nbsp;
expertise, Don wouldn’t do it himself and this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:42.900
the exact philosophy that Doug and Michael would&nbsp;
use overseas. Instead of investing themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:48.240
these two would assemble local teams in these&nbsp;
overseas countries specifically in China and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:55.740
India. Let’s just say, this strategy has worked&nbsp;
out spectacularly. In China, their most notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:03.600
investments include Alibaba, DJI, JD, Nio, and&nbsp;
of course Bytedance. And here’s a list of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:08.700
hundreds of investments in India. But, just&nbsp;
because Sequoia was expanding internationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:14.820
didn’t mean that they were slacking at home. In&nbsp;
fact, they were catching even bigger fish back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:21.900
home. In 1999, they would score Google and PayPal.&nbsp;
In 2003, they would score LinkedIn. In 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:27.360
they would score Kayak and YouTube. And in&nbsp;
2009, they would score DropBox which brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:33.180
us into modern times. Over the past 15 years,&nbsp;
the world has experienced a mobile revolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:40.080
which has progressed faster than anyone could have&nbsp;
ever imagined. But, somehow, Sequoia managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:46.440
stay on the leading edge of the mobile revolution&nbsp;
as well. Their first mobile investment was Airbnb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:53.340
in 2009 where they invested a total of $600,000.&nbsp;
They would follow this up with Evernote, Stripe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.340 --> 00:12:00.300
Tumblr, Square, WhatsApp, Instagram, Instacart,&nbsp;
Doordash, Reddit, Github, Zoom, Robinhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:06.300
Dashlane, Figma, and SpaceX just to name a few.&nbsp;
How are they able to find so many winners you ask?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:12.660
Well, it’s not rocket science though they invest&nbsp;
in rocket science as well. The secret is simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:18.420
that they’re constantly handing off the torch to&nbsp;
newer generation leaders. First, Don passed on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:23.760
torch to Doug and Michael who were responsible for&nbsp;
many of the dotcom and international hits. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:28.920
guys then passed the torch to Jim Goetz who scored&nbsp;
most of their victories throughout the 2000s. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:34.260
more recently, it seems that the torch has been&nbsp;
passed onto Matt Miller and Andrew Reed who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:41.340
both barely 30 years old. In fact, Andrew actually&nbsp;
identified Github and Robinhood in his 20s. And as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:46.200
long as Sequoia keeps embracing the youth, they&nbsp;
won’t be losing their own youth anytime soon.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:49.080
THE LEGACY OF SEQUOIA:&nbsp;

00:12:49.080 --> 00:13:00.300
Nowadays there’s a lot of VC firms out there that&nbsp;
have hit it big from Tiger Management to Andreesen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:07.140
Horowitz. But, what sets apart Sequoia is their&nbsp;
longevity. Most of the other VC firms have barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:13.200
been around for 15 to 20 years, but Sequoia&nbsp;
has been around for over 50. And the craziest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:17.520
part about all of this is that they were investing&nbsp;
in leading edge companies the entire time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:24.360
They’ve literally had their hands in everything&nbsp;
from Apple in the 1970s to SpaceX in the 2020s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:31.440
Unfortunately, Don Valentine wasn’t around to see&nbsp;
this as he passed away in late 2019 at age 87.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:38.340
But, while Don is no longer with us, there’s no&nbsp;
question that his legacy and impact will live on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:42.660
for as long as the Sequoia tree itself.&nbsp;
